{
  "dom_id": "step-q313985",
  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "A spotlight $S$ is on the ground $6\\,\\text{m}$ away from a wall and a $1.5\\,\\text{m}$ tall robot is moving towards the wall at a rate of $0.6\\,\\text{m/s}.$ To **one decimal place**, how fast is the height of the shadow on the wall decreasing when the robot is $2.5\\,\\text{m}$ from the spotlight?\n\nThe shadow decreases at a speed of approximately \n\n{{field-1}} $\\text{m/s}.$",
  "worked_solution": "Let's draw a figure according to the given data.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14209554/assets/e91c4e08aa785de1fe500c2f43e0981501ee597bb7bb042827154842ec06ad0c.png)\n\nIn the figure, $x$ is the robot’s distance from the wall, while $y$ is the length of the shadow. Both $x$ and $y$ change with time. In particular, we know that the robot’s velocity is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-0.6\\,\\text{m/s}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the robot’s velocity is *negative* because it is moving *toward* the wall, and therefore the distance between it and the wall *decreases* with time.\n\nSince we want to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we need to find a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Since the two triangles are similar, the ratios between the corresponding sides are equal, and we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{(6-x)+x} & =\\frac{1.5}{6-x} \\\\ \\frac{y}{6} & =\\frac{1.5}{6-x} \\\\ y & =\\frac{9}{6-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe differentiate both sides with respect to $t$ using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{9}{6-x}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{9}{(6-x)^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=6-2.5=3.5\\,\\text{m}$ and $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-0.6\\,\\text{m/s}$ into the above expression gives, to one decimal place\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{9}{{2.5}^{2}}\\cdot (-0.6) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & ≈-0.9\\,\\text{m/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shadow decreases at a speed of approximately  $0.9\\,\\text{m/s}.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
